<!--
GOLDEN CLAUDE.md (master). Contains every section. HTML comments are not sent to
Claude.

To use it in a project:
1. CLAUDE.md = the Project block plus the Part A sections that apply (see each
   "Applies when"), at 200 lines or fewer.
2. Each Part B section that applies becomes .claude/rules/<file> with a `paths:`
   frontmatter, so it loads only when Claude touches a matching file:
     Python                     python.md       "**/*.py"
     JavaScript and TypeScript  javascript.md   "**/*.js", "**/*.mjs", "**/*.ts"
     HTML and CSS               html-css.md     "**/*.html", "**/*.css"
     UI design                  ui-design.md    "**/*.html", "**/*.css", "**/*.js"
     HTTP APIs                  http-apis.md    "src/api/**"
     Data and storage           data.md         "data/**", "src/store/**"
     CLI apps                   cli.md          "main.py"
     Static-site builds         static-site.md  "content/**", "site/**", "scripts/build-site.js"
     MCP servers and hosts      mcp.md          "src/mcp/**"
     Docker                     docker.md       "Dockerfile", "compose.yaml", ".dockerignore"
   Adjust the paths to the project's layout in docs/design.md.
-->

# CLAUDE.md

## Project
<!-- C1. Fill in for each project. Claude changes this section only after the user approves (see Living docs). -->

- Purpose: {{PURPOSE}}
- Stack: {{STACK}}
- Test command: {{TEST_COMMAND}}
- Run command: {{RUN_COMMAND}}
- Project rules: {{PROJECT_RULES}} (rules that apply only to this project, including protected paths)

## Identity and source of truth
<!-- A1. Applies when: always. -->

The objective is clean, maintainable code for humans and machines. Humans can
read and change it. Machines (AI agents, type checkers, linters, tests) can
navigate and verify it.

All code is written by AI. Follow every rule in this file exactly.

- `docs/workflow.md`, `docs/inbox.md`, `docs/spec.md`, `docs/design.md` and
  `docs/plan.md` always exist. `docs/workflow.md` says what each one is for.
- Requirements live only in `docs/spec.md`. Never implement a requirement that
  exists only in chat, in code, or in memory.
- If a request is not covered by the spec, or contradicts it, stop and ask. Do
  not infer scope.
- Write the spec in EARS syntax, one requirement per statement:
  - Ubiquitous: `The <system> shall <response>.`
  - Event: `When <trigger>, the <system> shall <response>.`
  - State: `While <state>, the <system> shall <response>.`
  - Unwanted behavior: `If <condition>, then the <system> shall <response>.`
  - Optional feature: `Where <feature>, the <system> shall <response>.`
  - Complex: combine the patterns, for example `While <state>, when <trigger>, ...`.
- Every requirement has a stable ID `AREA-NNN` (for example `TRI-024`; `NFR-NNN`
  for non-functional). Every design decision has an ID `DEC-NNN` in
  `docs/design.md`. Never renumber or reuse an ID. Mark a retired requirement
  "withdrawn" and keep it.
- Do not copy spec or design content into this file or into code comments. Cite
  the ID instead.

## Workflow
<!-- A2. Applies when: always. Needs docs/workflow.md (master copy: workflow.md). -->

Follow the workflow in @docs/workflow.md exactly.

Do not refactor, rename, or move anything outside the current milestone.

## Communication and reporting
<!-- A3. Applies when: always. -->

- Be very concise and direct. No narration, no process commentary, no recap
  beyond the report below.
- Go top-down: the big picture first (what changed, why it matters), then the
  details.
- Handle one thing at a time: one item, one question, one suggestion per
  message.
- When you ask the user to decide, give a recommendation and the pros and cons
  of each choice, very briefly.
- When discussing requirements, design, the plan, or an approach the user
  proposes, act as a rigorous critical-thinking partner: challenge assumptions,
  expose weaknesses and blind spots, test the reasoning, present alternatives,
  and agree only when the argument is strong.
  - In the spec, act as a product manager.
  - In the design, act as a software architect.
- While executing an approved milestone, do not debate. Flag only blockers and
  spec conflicts.

End every task that changes files with this report, each item on one line:

- **Files changed:** a list.
- **Tests added or updated:** a list.
- **Tests run:** N passed out of M.
- **Assumptions and open questions:** very concise.
- **TLDR: what I need to know if I read nothing else.** (last, whole line in bold)

## Code smells
<!-- A5. Applies when: always. -->

- Each function does one thing. If its name needs two verbs, split it.
- Target 20 lines or fewer per function body, not counting the docstring. Split
  any function that is longer.
- Give a function 4 parameters or fewer. Group values that always travel
  together into one typed object. Inject long-lived collaborators through the
  constructor, not through every call.
- Each class and module does one thing. If describing it needs "and", extract a
  class. When a file passes about 300 lines, check it for a second
  responsibility and split it only if you find one.
- Prefer composition to inheritance.
- Do not write:
  - duplicated logic (search for an existing helper first; extract on the
    second use);
  - dead code;
  - abstractions or design patterns the current task does not need;
  - pass-through wrappers that only forward to another function or class, or
    classes too small to justify themselves (the single owner of an external
    system is not a pass-through);
  - mutable global state.
- Write code a beginner-to-intermediate programmer can read. Prefer plain
  constructs to clever ones.
- Never disable a linter, a type check, or a test to make a change pass. Fix the
  code.

## Architecture and layers
<!-- A6. Applies when: always. -->

- The layers and their import rules are defined in `docs/design.md`. Follow
  them.
- Before writing code, name the module that owns the change. Before creating a
  module, state its name, layer, and single responsibility.
- One module per concern. No monolithic files.
- Entry points (the main script, the app factory, the front-end main module)
  only import and connect modules. They hold no logic.
- Business logic is pure: it imports no HTTP, UI, or database code. Transport
  and UI layers only translate and delegate.
- Exactly one module talks to each external system (a file area, a database, a
  network API, an LLM). Everything else goes through it.
- A module exposes only what other modules need. It never reaches into another
  module's internals or state.
- Pass anything that touches an external system (repository, API client, file
  writer, LLM client) into the code that uses it, so tests can use fakes. Do not
  use a dependency-injection framework.
- Changing a layer, a module boundary, the data model, the public API, or the
  tech stack needs the user's approval. Record it as a `DEC-NNN` in
  `docs/design.md`.

## Directory structure
<!-- A7. Applies when: always. A host that needs files at the repo root (for example GitHub Pages) lists the exception in docs/design.md. -->

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `src/` | All source code. `docs/design.md` has the detailed layout. |
| `tests/` | All tests. |
| `docs/` | `workflow.md`, `inbox.md`, `spec.md`, `design.md`, `plan.md`, and reference material (mockups, source documents). |
| `config/` | `.env` (never committed) and `.env.example` (committed). |
| `data/` | Input and runtime data. |
| `content/` | Markdown source for a static site. Authors edit only this. |
| `site/` | Generated static site, committed for hosting. Never edited by hand. |
| `models/` | Downloaded model weights, when a local model loads from disk. Never committed. Path comes from config (`MODELS_DIR`). |
| `prompts/` | LLM prompt files, when the project calls an LLM. |
| `scripts/` | Setup, download, and maintenance scripts, including `cleanup.py`, `download_models.py`, and `download_assets.py`. |
| `.vscode/` | Shared editor settings. |
| `.claude/` | Claude Code project settings. |
| Root files | `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `requirements.txt`, `.gitignore`, `main.py` (the entry point, Python), and `Dockerfile`, `compose.yaml`, `.dockerignore` (Docker). |

- All source code lives under `src/`, except the thin entry point `main.py` at
  the root.
- Create a folder only when the layout in `docs/design.md` lists it. Update the
  layout in the same change.
- Never edit a generated file by hand. Regenerate it with the script that makes
  it.
- `scripts/cleanup.py` removes caches and generated artifacts from the repo
  (for example `__pycache__`, tool caches, logs), by name, wherever they sit. It
  never deletes source, tests, docs, configuration, input data, or dependencies.

## Naming and code documentation
<!-- A8. Applies when: always. Name formats and docstring syntax are in the language sections. -->

- Name things for their purpose, not for how they look or how they are built.
  Use one term per concept, and keep naming consistent across the codebase.
- Every module, class, and function has a docstring. Tests are exempt: their
  names carry the requirement ID.
  - Module: what it does, what it does not do (and which module does), and the
    requirement or decision IDs it implements.
  - Class: its single responsibility, stated explicitly, and the state it owns,
    if any.
  - Public function: a one-line summary, then its parameters, return value, and
    errors raised.
  - Private or trivial function: a one-line summary.
- Comment only where the behavior is not obvious. Do not narrate the code.
- Do not leave TODO comments. Report out-of-scope findings under "Assumptions
  and open questions".

## Configuration and constants
<!-- A9. Applies when: the project has configurable values. Secrets: see Security and privacy. -->

- Environment settings live in `config/.env`, which is never committed.
  `config/.env.example` is committed and lists every key. Non-secret keys carry
  their default; secret keys are empty.
- `config.py` is the only module that reads `config/.env` or the environment. A
  real environment variable overrides `config/.env`.
- Only the entry point imports `config`. It passes values down as arguments,
  grouped into small typed settings objects per component. No other module reads
  the environment.
- The code has no defaults. A missing or invalid value fails clearly at startup.
- Never write a behavior-affecting value inline: URLs, ports, file paths, model
  names, thresholds, limits, timeouts. They come from config.
- Fixed vocabularies (statuses, sources, error codes, review reasons) are enums,
  or named immutable constants where the language has no enums, in the module
  that owns them. No other module-level constants.
- To add a setting, in the same change:
  1. Add the key to `config/.env.example` (its default, or empty if secret).
  2. Add the key to `config/.env`.
  3. Add a typed field to the settings in `config.py`.
  4. Add a row to the settings table in `docs/design.md`.
  5. Pass the value in from the entry point.

## Dependencies
<!-- A10. Applies when: the project uses third-party libraries. Environment commands are in the language sections. -->

- Prefer the standard library. Ask before adding any dependency, and say why the
  standard library is not enough.
- Install only into the project's isolated environment. Never install into the
  global interpreter or a base environment.
- The dependency file (`requirements.txt`; `package.json` for JavaScript) is the
  only list of dependencies.
- Add a library to it in the same change that first imports it. Never import a
  library that is not listed. Remove it when its last import goes.
- List direct dependencies only, pinned with `==` to the version installed when
  it was added. Do not use `pip freeze`.
- Use only APIs that exist in the pinned version.

## Testing
<!-- A11. Applies when: the project has automated tests. Test tools and naming syntax are in the language sections. -->

- Every requirement ID has a test at the lowest level that proves it.
- Every user workflow (an event-driven requirement, `When <trigger>, ...`) has an
  end-to-end test. End-to-end tests drive the outermost non-browser entry point
  (the HTTP API, the CLI, or the service), with fakes at the external
  boundaries. The UI is built by AI and never read by the user, so every
  requirement that describes a UI workflow also gets one headless-browser test
  in `tests/e2e/ui/`, run as one of the loop checks. `docs/design.md` chooses
  the browser tool, and the user approves it under Dependencies.
- Every new public function in the business-logic layer also has a unit test.
- Layout: `tests/unit/` for logic with fakes, `tests/e2e/` for workflows. One
  unit-test file per source module.
- Requirement coverage is the primary measure. Also measure line and branch
  coverage on `src/`, with a floor of 80%, as one of the checks in the workflow
  loop.
- Test public behavior, not private implementation. One behavior per test. Every
  test asserts something.
- Tests are deterministic: seed randomness, no real clock, network, or sleeps,
  and assert exact outputs.
- Tests use temporary folders and invented data. They never touch real config,
  data, or user files, and never need a live LLM or external server.
- Never weaken or edit an existing test, baseline, or fixture to make it pass.
  Change one only when its requirement changed, and say so in the report.

## Error handling
<!-- A12. Applies when: always. Exception syntax is in the language sections. -->

- Catch an exception only at an external boundary (file system, network,
  database, LLM, subprocess), and only to add context (what was being done, on
  which resource) and re-raise with the original error attached. Never catch to
  recover, fall back, or continue.
- Never use a bare `except`. Never swallow an error.
- Do not catch unexpected errors. The top level shows the raw error and the full
  stack trace, logs it, and stops. No friendly message, no retry.
- Invalid user input is not an error. Handle it as Input validation says.
- Error messages name what failed and the resource (path, endpoint, ID). They
  never contain secrets or user content.
- If other users can reach the app, `docs/design.md` records a `DEC-NNN` that
  shows a short message and logs the trace instead.

## Logging
<!-- A13. Applies when: the project runs as an app or service. -->

- Use the language's logging library for diagnostics. Never use `print` for
  them. A CLI may `print` its intended user output.
- Configure logging once, in the entry point, from settings. The log level and
  the log file path come from config. Log to the console and to the file.
- Write plain text, one line per event, with timestamp, level, and module name.
- Log startup (with the settings in use, secrets excluded), key events, and
  failures.
- Levels: `INFO` for startup and key events, `DEBUG` for payloads and raw
  external responses, `WARNING` for rejected input, `ERROR` for failures.
- Never log secrets. Log user content only at `DEBUG`.
- Log an error once, at the top level. Do not log and re-raise at a boundary.

## LLMs and prompts
<!-- A14. Applies when: the project calls an LLM. The LLM client module, model names in config, raw responses at DEBUG, and no live LLM in tests are covered in Architecture, Configuration, Logging, and Testing. -->

- A project uses one provider, chosen in `docs/design.md`. Do not build a
  provider switch unless the spec requires one.
- Prompts live in files under `prompts/`, never as strings in code. The path to
  each prompt file comes from config.
- The model ID, base URL, models folder path, and sampling and token limits come
  from config. Use an exact model ID, not a moving alias.
- Treat LLM output as untrusted input. Parse it and validate it against a
  schema. If it fails, raise an error that includes the raw output. Do not
  repair it and do not retry.
- The LLM only produces data. It never executes tools, runs code, or decides
  transport or server behavior. Code executes the tools.
- API-hosted model: data leaves the machine. Send only the fields the prompt
  needs. `docs/design.md` states which data goes to which provider.
- Local model: the base URL points at localhost. Fail clearly when the server is
  not running or the model is not pulled. Download models only with
  `scripts/download_models.py`, never at run time. At run time the app is
  offline, with telemetry off. Weights live in `models/` and are never
  committed.
- The README explains how to set up and check the LLM (server, model, API key)
  before the first run.

## Git and destructive actions
<!-- A15. Applies when: always. Commit and push timing is in docs/workflow.md. -->

- Work on `main` unless told otherwise.
- Never commit `config/.env`, secrets, user data, model weights, exports, logs,
  or caches. Before the first commit, check that `.gitignore` covers them.
- Never run `git push --force`, `git reset --hard`, `git clean`, or any
  `git checkout` or `git restore` that discards changes.
- Never delete recursively (`rm -rf`, `rmdir /s`, `Remove-Item -Recurse`),
  except a scratch folder you made in this milestone. Clear caches with
  `scripts/cleanup.py`.
- Never touch files outside this repo, except scratch folders you make and
  delete. Never touch the paths listed as protected under Project rules. Never
  kill another program's process.
- If a task needs a forbidden action, or an approval you cannot get now (a new
  dependency, an architecture change, a spec change), stop that milestone. Write
  the reason and the exact question to `.claude/blocked.md`, and ask in chat if
  the user is present. `.claude/blocked.md` is never committed. The user clears
  it.

## Security and privacy
<!-- A16. Applies when: always. -->

- Secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords, database IDs) live only in `config/.env`
  or the platform's secret store. They never appear in code, committed files,
  logs, error messages, reports, or browser-side code.
- User data stays on the machine. A flow of user data to an external service (an
  LLM API, Notion, a cloud store) must be listed in `docs/design.md` as a
  `DEC-NNN` and approved by the user.
- Servers listen on `127.0.0.1` unless `docs/design.md` says otherwise.
  Telemetry is off. At run time the app calls only the services
  `docs/design.md` lists.
- Never run, test, or debug against the user's real data. Copy it to a scratch
  folder, point the process at the copy through environment variables of that
  process only, and delete the copy afterwards. Reports give counts, never
  content.
- Never put real personal data (names, figures, IDs) in source, fixtures, or
  docs. Use invented data.

## Living docs
<!-- A17. Applies when: always. -->

- Update every doc a change makes inaccurate in the same change. Be specific:
  "Added `domain/review.py`: computes Needs Review", not "updated code".
- Each doc owns one kind of fact. Link to the owner; do not copy.

  | Change | Update |
  | --- | --- |
  | Behavior or a requirement | `docs/spec.md`, with the user's approval |
  | Architecture, data model, settings, directory layout, rules, thresholds | `docs/design.md` |
  | Setup, how to run, user-visible changes | `README.md` |
  | A library added or removed | `requirements.txt`, in the same change as the import (see Dependencies) |
  | New generated files, caches, secrets | `.gitignore` |

- `README.md` always has these sections: What it does, Stack (with versions),
  File structure (top-level folders, one line each), Setup, How to run, How to
  test. Setup explains how to install dependencies, configure `config/.env`, and
  set up and check every external dependency (for example the LLM server,
  models, API keys) before the first run.
- Never edit `CLAUDE.md` without the user's approval. When a fact in it goes
  stale (stack, test command, run command) or a rule needs to change, flag it,
  propose the exact change, and edit only after approval.

## Long-running work
<!-- A18. Applies when: the project has tasks that can run long (network or LLM calls, large files, pipelines). -->

- Long-running work never blocks the UI or the request path. Use async I/O for
  waits, and keep blocking or CPU-heavy work off the main loop.
- Python: use `async def` and `await` for I/O at the boundaries (API handlers,
  LLM and network clients). Pure business logic stays synchronous. Never call a
  blocking function inside `async def`; run it with `asyncio.to_thread`. Async
  tests use `pytest-asyncio`.
- JavaScript: use `async`/`await` with `fetch`. Move heavy computation to a Web
  Worker. Never freeze the main thread.
- An API request that can run long starts the task and returns `202` with a job
  ID at once. The task runs in the background as an async task.
  `GET /api/jobs/<id>` reports its status and progress, and the UI polls it. One
  object created in `main.py` holds the job state.

## Input validation
<!-- A19. Applies when: the project accepts user input (forms, API requests, command-line arguments, uploaded files). -->

- Every user input passes through a validation component (a class or module) at
  the boundary, before any logic uses it. The validation rules live only there,
  never in handlers, views, or business logic.
- The component checks type, required fields, length or range, format, and
  allowed values. It reports every problem at once, naming the field and the
  rule, never the rejected value.
- Valid input leaves the component as a typed object. Invalid input is rejected
  with a clear message about what to fix. It is not an error.
- The server-side component is the authority. The browser may give fast feedback
  with native HTML constraint attributes (`required`, `type`, `min`, `max`,
  `pattern`), and a small validation-view module in JavaScript shows the
  server's field errors next to the fields. It does not copy the rules.
- Python: use a Pydantic model for API bodies and a validator class for other
  inputs. Each rule has a test.

## Python
<!-- B1. Applies when: the project uses Python. -->

- Naming: `snake_case` for modules, functions, and variables; `PascalCase` for
  classes; `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for enum members. Test files are
  `test_<module>.py`. Test functions are `test_<requirement id>_<behavior>`, for
  example `test_tri_024_unsure_duplicate_creates_item`.
- Types: annotate every parameter and return value, every class attribute, and
  any variable whose type is not obvious. Use built-in generics and `|`
  (`list[str]`, `str | None`), not `typing.List` or `Optional`. No `Any` unless
  a library forces it; then add a comment saying why.
- Use dataclasses for typed records. Settings are frozen dataclasses.
- Docstrings use Google style with `Args:`, `Returns:`, and `Raises:` sections.
  The first line is a one-line summary that ends with a period.
- Imports go at the top of the file only, as `from src.<module> import <name>`.
  Every folder under `src/` has an `__init__.py`.
- Use f-strings. Use `with` for resources. Do not write your own decorators,
  metaclasses, context managers, or descriptors. Library decorators such as
  `@dataclass` and `@pytest.fixture` are fine.
- Prefix internal names with `_`.
- Open text files with `encoding="utf-8"`. Use `pathlib.Path`, not path strings.
- Re-raise with `raise ... from err`.
- A module may define `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`. It is the only
  module-level object besides enums.
- `main.py` at the repo root is the entry point. It loads config, sets up
  logging, creates the objects, and starts the app. It holds no logic.
- `config.py` loads `config/.env` with `python-dotenv`.
- Use the project's conda environment (its name and Python version are in the
  README Stack). Conda supplies Python only. Install packages with
  `pip install -r requirements.txt`, never `conda install`.
- Checks. Run all of them and fix every failure:
  - `mypy src`
  - `ruff check .` and `ruff format --check .`
  - `python -m pytest --cov=src --cov-branch --cov-fail-under=80`
- `requirements.txt` lists the check tools: `pytest`, `pytest-cov`, `mypy`,
  `ruff`, and `python-dotenv`, plus `pytest-asyncio` for async code.

## JavaScript and TypeScript
<!-- B2. Applies when: the project has JavaScript or TypeScript. -->

- No front-end framework (React, Vue, Angular, and the like) and no UI library,
  unless `docs/design.md` chooses one. HTML, CSS, and JavaScript stay in
  separate files.
- Default: plain JavaScript as ES modules, typed with JSDoc and checked by the
  TypeScript compiler. Start every file with `// @ts-check`. `tsconfig.json`
  sets `checkJs` and `strict`. Write TypeScript source only if `docs/design.md`
  chooses a build toolchain. Either way, write no `any`.
- The runtime has no dependencies and no build step. A dev-only `package.json`
  holds `typescript` and `eslint`. Unit tests use `node --test`.
- Naming: `kebab-case` files, `camelCase` functions and variables, `PascalCase`
  classes.
- Every function has JSDoc with `@param {Type}` and `@returns {Type}`, even when
  it is not exported. These are the types. Describe a shared object shape once
  with `@typedef`. Every module starts with an `@module` block.
- Structure mirrors the Architecture rules. `main.js` only imports and connects
  modules. Keep logic modules (no DOM, no `fetch`) apart from view modules (each
  owns its own DOM) and `api.js` (the only module that calls `fetch`). Export
  only the public API. Modules talk through function calls or custom events,
  never by reaching into another module's DOM or state.
- Pass collaborators (the api module, storage, the clock) into the functions
  that use them, so tests can use fakes.
- No mutable module-level state: no module-level `let` and no mutable
  module-level objects. Create state in `main.js` and pass it in.
- Behavior-affecting values (URLs, timeouts, limits) live in one `config.js`
  that exports a frozen object. Fixed vocabularies are frozen objects in the
  module that owns them.
- Errors: `try/catch` only in `api.js`, adding context and rethrowing with
  `new Error(message, { cause })`. Unexpected errors reach a top-level handler in
  `main.js` (the `error` and `unhandledrejection` events) that shows the raw
  error and stack trace on screen and logs it.
- Logging: one `log.js` wraps `console` with levels taken from config. No other
  module calls `console`.
- JavaScript holds behavior only: no HTML markup strings and no styles. Clone
  `<template>` elements from the HTML or use `createElement`, and toggle CSS
  classes. Insert dynamic text with `textContent`; never build HTML from data.
- Never keep app data in `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`; the API is the only
  source of data. UI preferences may use `localStorage`.
- ESLint enforces the code-smell limits: 20 lines per function, 4 parameters,
  and complexity as errors, and 300 lines per file as a warning. Do not disable
  a rule.
- Checks. Run all of them and fix every failure: `npx tsc --noEmit`,
  `npx eslint .`, `node --test`, and the headless-browser tests (the command is
  in the README under How to test).
- The UI is built and maintained entirely by AI. Report front-end changes by
  their behavior in plain language, and prove correctness with the checks.

## HTML and CSS
<!-- B3. Applies when: the project has a browser front end. -->

- Use semantic elements (`header`, `main`, `nav`, `aside`, `article`, `section`,
  `footer`). Every page has a clear `<title>` and one main heading.
- No `style=""` attributes and no `<style>` blocks. Pass computed values to CSS
  through custom properties set from JavaScript.
- No `onclick=""` or other inline handlers, and no `<script>` blocks, except the
  module entry in `index.html`. Attach behavior in JavaScript, using event
  delegation with `data-` attributes.
- HTML holds structure only. Markup that JavaScript needs lives in `<template>`
  elements in the HTML, not in JavaScript strings.
- Nothing loads from the network: no CDN, no Google Fonts. Store third-party
  files in the repo. A required external script is listed in `docs/design.md`.
- Colors, spacing, and fonts come only from custom properties in `theme.css`.
  Never write a color value in any other file.
- Keep shared sheets (`theme.css`, base, layout) and add one sheet per view or
  component only for styles specific to it. Every CSS file starts with a header
  comment that says what it styles and what it does not.
- Name CSS classes with BEM, `block__element--modifier`, and by purpose, not
  appearance (`sidebar__toggle`, not `blue-box`).
- Avoid duplicate rules, unused classes, excessive specificity, and layout
  hacks.
- Use meaningful link text and `alt` text on images. Keep text contrast
  readable. Do not rely on color alone to convey meaning.
- Mockups in `docs/` are a visual reference, not code to copy.

## UI design
<!-- B3a. Applies when: the project has a user interface. -->

- Quick: no unnecessary scripts, fonts, or images. Every action gives immediate
  feedback, and slow work shows a loading state.
- Responsive: the layout adapts from phone to wide desktop widths without
  horizontal scrolling, and content keeps a readable width on large screens.
- Minimal and uncluttered: show only what the current task needs, with one
  primary action per view, few colors, and generous whitespace. No decorative
  effects and no complex animation.
- Standard layout: header, navigation, main content, and footer, in the usual
  places. Use native HTML controls (`button`, `a`, `input`, `select`) instead of
  custom widgets.
- Every button and icon control has hover text (`title`) that explains what it
  does. Hover is not the only explanation: an icon-only control also has an
  `aria-label`, because touch screens have no hover.
- Prefer the system font stack. Add a custom font or an image only when
  `docs/design.md` requires it. Download it with `scripts/download_assets.py`
  into `src/assets/fonts/` or `src/assets/images/`, and commit the files. Never
  link to a font or an image online. The script records each source URL and
  license, and only assets whose license allows redistribution are used.

## HTTP APIs
<!-- B4. Applies when: the project exposes an HTTP API. -->

- Use FastAPI. Handlers are `async def`. The server serves the front end as
  static files and exposes a JSON API. No server-side HTML templating.
- A route handler calls one service function and returns the response. It holds
  no business logic. One router per domain.
- API paths are `kebab-case` plural nouns under `/api/`, for example
  `/api/triage-options`. Use GET to read, POST to create, PATCH to update, and
  DELETE to delete. A `/health` endpoint reports liveness.
- A Pydantic model validates every request body (see Input validation). Invalid
  input returns FastAPI's standard 422 `detail`, one entry per problem. Use 404
  for a missing record and 409 for a conflict, both with a `detail` message.
- Authentication goes through one shared dependency or decorator, used by every
  protected endpoint. `docs/design.md` lists the public endpoints.
- Allowed CORS origins come from config. The default is none.
- End-to-end tests call the API through the framework's test client against
  temporary data. They do not start a server.

## Data and storage
<!-- B5. Applies when: the project reads or writes data files or a data store. -->

- `data/` holds input and runtime data. Code never lives in `data/`, and data
  never lives in `src/`.
- Raw input is read-only. Never modify or overwrite an input file. Generated
  output goes to a separate folder under `data/`, and a script can regenerate
  it. Caches are disposable.
- Exactly one module reads and writes each data store or file format. It parses
  and validates at that boundary and hands typed objects to the rest of the
  code. No other module parses raw fields.
- The data folder and file paths come from config. Tests use temporary folders.
- Document the data model in `docs/design.md`, and update it in the same change
  that changes the data.
- Use UUID strings for IDs and ISO 8601 UTC strings for timestamps.
- Before a change rewrites stored data in place (a migration, an import), copy
  the data to `<name>.before-<change>`. Never delete these backups.

## CLI apps
<!-- B6. Applies when: the project has a command-line interface. -->

- Parse arguments with `argparse` in `main.py`, then dispatch to functions. Each
  flag and step is documented in the README under How to run.
- Print only the intended user output (results and progress). Diagnostics go to
  the logger. A progress counter may rewrite one line with `\r` and show counts
  only.
- Exit with code 0 on success and a non-zero code on failure. Unexpected errors
  follow Error handling.

## Static-site builds (Node)
<!-- B7. Applies when: the project generates a static site from Markdown. -->

- Markdown in `content/` is the source of truth. Authors edit only `content/`.
- `site/` is generated output, committed for hosting. Never edit it by hand.
  Regenerate it with `node scripts/build-site.js`. The hand-written template,
  CSS, and JavaScript live in `src/`, and the build copies them into `site/`.
- The build script generates every page from one reusable template. It builds
  the navigation from the content (never hardcoded links), uses relative links
  only, and names each output file after the content file's slug.
- The build fails clearly when required metadata is missing, and it skips
  drafts.
- `marked` is a build-time dependency only. The generated site has no runtime
  dependency and renders no Markdown in the browser.
- The site works without JavaScript for basic reading and navigation.
- Static files only: no server-side routing, and no environment variables or
  secrets in browser code.
- The README explains how to add content, build, preview locally, and publish.

## MCP servers and hosts
<!-- B8. Applies when: the project is an MCP server or an MCP host. -->

- Use the official MCP Python SDK. A server exposes tools over MCP, never
  through ad-hoc REST. A host calls tools through an MCP client, never through
  custom endpoints.
- Transport: Streamable HTTP on `127.0.0.1`. Use stdio only when the client
  launches the server itself. Do not use SSE. The server URL and port come from
  config.
- Each tool has a typed input schema and a typed result. Tool behavior is
  deterministic: no LLM inside a server.
- A host checks that the target tool exists, and handles connection and
  tool-call failures with explicit errors. The model never executes a tool. The
  MCP client does (see LLMs and prompts).
- The MCP client and server calls are async (see Long-running work).
- End-to-end tests cover server startup, session initialization, tool
  discovery, tool invocation, and the side effects of each call.

## Docker
<!-- B9. Applies when: the project uses Docker. -->

- Docker runs the services the app depends on (a database, a vector store) and
  is used for deployment. During development the app runs from its conda
  environment.
- Never run `docker compose down -v`, `docker volume rm`,
  `docker volume prune`, or `docker system prune`. Docker volumes are protected
  data.
- Published ports bind to `127.0.0.1`, for example `127.0.0.1:8000:8000`.
- Pin the base image tag. Never use `latest`.
- Never bake secrets or data into an image. They arrive at run time through
  environment variables and mounted volumes.
- `.dockerignore` excludes `.env`, `data/`, `models/`, and `.git`.
- Turn off telemetry in containers (for example `DISABLE_TELEMETRY=true`).
